+### AD-26: Feature information gain by permutation in walk-forward ridge and gradient-boosted models
+**Status:** Accepted · **Date:** 2026-10-18 · **Scope:** `lab`
+- **Context:** SIG-05 needs each feature's information gain over the 5-day residual return measured with ML models, fitted only on training windows (WFWD-04) and tested against chance like every other S3 claim. S2 uses no ML, and S4's torch stack lives only in `models` (AD-01).
+- **Decision:** Per walk-forward period, `lab.significance` fits a ridge regression (scikit-learn) and a LightGBM gradient-boosted tree on the training window (`training_window(horizon=5)`, inputs through `engine.guard`) to predict `fwd_resid_5d` from every candidate feature, cross-sectionally standardised per date. A feature's per-period gain is the drop in the models' mean out-of-sample rank IC on the test period when that feature's column is permuted across stocks, seeded from the registration. Each feature's gain series is its own `SIG` entry, tested against zero with the `null-hypothesis-testing` skill and corrected across the whole `SIG` family with the rank-IC entries. A feature survives (SIG-03) if either of its entries passes.
+- **Alternatives:** Drop-column refits — a refit per feature per period, ten times the cost, for a similar answer. Neural models — would put torch into `lab`, against AD-01. SHAP values — attribute the fitted model, not out-of-sample gain.
+- **Consequences:** `lab` gains scikit-learn and LightGBM, both CPU-only and seeded (QR-07). The `SIG` family doubles in size, so each test faces a stricter correction.
+- **Probe:** none — no external service involved.
